"H6720":"provision","H6213":"do","H4303":"delicacy","H834":"which","H157":"lover","H935":"come","H398":"to eat","H5668":"for the sake of","H1288":"to bless","H5315":"soul","H2962":"before","H4191":"to die","H7259":"Rebekah","H8085":"hear","H1696":"speak","H3212":"to walk","H6718":"wild game","H3290":"Jacob","H1":"father","H251":"male-sibling","H6440":"before","H3068":"LORD","H6963":"voice","H589":"I","H6680":"to command","H6629":"flock","H3947":"take","H8033":"there","H8147":"two","H1423":"kid","H5795":"goat","H2896":"pleasant","H517":"mother","H2005":"look!","H376":"man","H8163":"hairy","H595":"I","H2509":"smooth","H194":"perhaps","H4959":"to feel","H8591":"to mock","H5921":"upon","H7045":"curse","H1293":"blessing","H389":"surely","H899":"treachery","H2532":"desire","H854":"with","H1004":"place","H3847":"to clothe","H6996":"small","H5785":"skin","H3027":"hand","H2513":"portion","H6677":"neck","H5414":"give","H3899":"food","H4310":"who?","H859":"you(m.s.)","H1060":"firstborn","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H3427":"to dwell","H4100":"what?","H2088":"this","H4116":"to hasten","H4672":"to find","H7136":"to meet","H430":"God","H5066":"approach","H4184":"to feel","H518":"if","H5234":"to recognize","H4616":"because","H3196":"wine","H8354":"to drink","H5401":"to kiss","H7306":"to smell","H7381":"aroma","H2919":"dew","H8064":"heaven","H4924":"fat","H776":"country/planet","H7230":"abundance","H1715":"grain","H8492":"new wine","H5647":"to serve","H5971":"people","H7812":"to bow","H3816":"people","H1933":"to fall","H1376":"lord","H779":"to curse","H3615":"finish","H1571":"also","H1931":"he/she/it","H2729":"to tremble","H2731":"trembling","H5704":"till","H3966":"much","H645":"then","H3605":"all","H1697":"word","H6817":"to cry","H6818":"cry","H4751":"bitter","H4820":"deceit","H8034":"name","H6117":"to assail","H6471":"beat","H1062":"birthright","H680":"to reserve","H6030":"to dwell","H7760":"make","H5650":"servant/slave","H5564":"to support","H259":"one","H1058":"to weep","H4186":"seat","H2719":"sword","H2421":"to live","H7300":"to roam","H6561":"to tear","H5923":"yoke","H7852":"to hate","H3820":"heart","H7126":"come","H60":"mourning","H2026":"to kill","H5046":"to tell","H7971":"depart","H5162":"comfort","H1272":"to flee","H3837":"Laban","H2771":"Haran","H5973":"with","H7725":"return","H2534":"rage","H639":"anger","H4480":"from","H7911":"to forget","H7921":"be bereaved","H6973":"to loathe","H2416":"alive","H1323":"daughter","H2845":"Heth","H802":"woman","H428":"these"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Old: 137 years, when falling sickly and blind, at least for a time, he wished to bless Esau, who was 77 years old. (Tirinus)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","That, &c. He does not mean, that the meat would induce him to give his blessing. Neither can we suppose, that he intended to pervert the order of God, in making the younger son subject to the elder, if he was informed by Rebecca, of that disposition of providence. (Calmet) — But of this he seems to have been ignorant, ver. 29, 35. (Worthington)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","In the sight of the Lord, answers to my soul, &c., ver. 4. I will bless thee with all earnestness and sincerity. (Haydock)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Mocked him, taking advantage of his blindness and old age. (Menochius)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","This curse. Rebecca had too much confidence in God’s promises, to think that he would suffer them to be ineffectual. Hence, Onkelos makes her say, “I have learnt by revelation, that thou wilt receive no curse, but only blessing.” The sequel shewed, that she was directed by God in this delicate business. (Theodoret q. 78.) (Calmet)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Very good. Hebrew desirable, kept among perfumes, ver. 27. Such, the Hebrews say, were used by the first-born, when they offered sacrifice. (St. Jerome, q. Hebrews.)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","I am Esau, thy first-born. St. Augustine, (L. Contra Mendacium, c. x.) treating at large upon this place, excuseth Jacob from a lie, because this whole passage was mysterious, as relating to the preference which was afterwards to be given to the Gentiles before the carnal Jews, which Jacob by prophetic light might understand. So far is certain, that the first birth-right, both by divine election, and by Esau’s free cession, belonged to Jacob: so that if there were any lie in the case, it could be no more than an officious and venial one. (Challoner) — Ignorance might also excuse them from any sin; as many good and learned men have thought an officious lie to be lawful. (St. Chrysostom, hom. 52; Origen; Bonfrere.) And even if we allow that they did wrong; the Scripture relates, but does not sanction what they did, Let him that thinks himself to stand, take heed, lest he fall, 1 Corinthians x. 12. (Calmet) — As our Saviour says of St. John the Baptist, He is Elias, Matthew xi, so, Jacob says, I am Esau, not in person , but in right of the first-born. (Worthington)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Of Esau. Thus, too often our voice contradicts our hands or actions! (Haydock)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Plentiful. A word retained by the Samaritan and Septuagint though lost in the Hebrew copies. (Grotius.) — Hath blessed with abundance of fruit and odoriferous herbs; such as had probably been shut up in the drawers with Esau’s robes. (Menochius)"],["haydock",28,null,"augustine","Wine. “By which Christ gathers together the multitude, in the Sacrament of his Body and Blood.” (St. Augustine)"],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Worship thee, with civil respect, (Haydock) as the Idumeans, Philistines and Moabites did, with respect to David, Solomon, and the Machabees, acknowledging their dominion, though reluctantly. — With blessing. Thus Rebecca had not given her son a vain assurance. Isaac prays that God may ever be his protector, and avenge his cause. (Haydock)"],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Fear. Septuagint, “Isaac was wrapt into an ecstasy exceedingly great;” during which God explained to him the meaning of what had happened, that he might not think of revoking his blessing. (St. Augustine, q. 80.) He permitted Isaac to be in darkness respecting this affair, that it might be more manifest, that the will of man had no part in preferring Jacob; (St. Chrysostom, hom. 53.) and that Esau might not direct his rage against his father. (Worthington)"],["haydock",33,null,"haydock","Be blessed. Thus he confirms what he had done; and shews that he bore no resentment towards his younger son, nor esteemed himself to be mocked, ver. 12. (Haydock)"],["haydock",34,null,"haydock","Roared, through savage fury and envy of his brother. (Eusebius) (Menochius)"],["haydock",35,null,"haydock","Deceitfully. Hebrew, slily; directed by wisdom, as the Chaldean has it. St. Chrysostom (de sacerd.) praises the address of Jacob on this occasion. (Calmet)"],["haydock",36,null,"haydock","Jacob. That is, a supplanter. (Challoner) — My blessing. Both Isaac and Esau speak of this blessing, according to the dictates of nature. But God had disposed of it otherwise. The profane and cruel manners of Esau rendered him unworthy of it; and he could not maintain his natural claim, after having freely resigned it even with an oath. He seems to distinguish the blessing from the birth-right, though one necessarily followed the other. (Haydock)"],["haydock",37,null,"haydock","Brethren, or relations; (Menochius) for Isaac had no other children but these two. He never married any other woman but the beautiful and virtuous Rebecca. (Haydock)"],["haydock",39,null,"haydock","Moved; yet not so as to repent of what he had done; for Esau found no place of repentance in his father’s breast, although with tears he had sought it, (Hebrews xii. 17.) desiring to obtain the blessing of the first-born. (Haydock) — In the fat, &c. Idumea was a barren country; and hence some would translate the Hebrew, “far from the fat…shall thy dwelling be; but thou shalt live by the sword.” Thus min often means from, as well as for in: my flesh is changed on account of the want of oil, Psalm cviii. 24. Hebrew, a pinguedine. (Calmet) — But all the ancient versions agree with the Vulgate. So that we may say, the blessing of God made those barren regions supply the wants of the people abundantly; and as the Idumeans were to live by the sword, they would seize the rich habitations of their neighbours, (Haydock) and thus obtain a country rendered fertile without their labour. (Menochius)"],["haydock",40,null,"haydock","Thy brother, in the reign of David, 2 Kings viii. 14, and of the Machabees. (Josephus, Antiquities xiii. 17.) — Yoke. When the house of Juda shall rebel against the Lord, in the days of Joram, then the Idumeans shall regain their liberty for a time; (4 Kings viii. 20.) to be subdued again after 800 years by John Hyrcan, the high priest. (Haydock) — All the blessing of Esau, tends to confirm that already given to his brother; so that the apostle seems to have considered it unworthy of notice. (Calmet) — Jacob, in the mean time, never asserted his dominion; but still called Esau his lord, (Chap. xxxii. 4.) and behaved to him with the greatest deference. (Haydock) — Yet the Idumeans always hated the Jews, and assisted Titus to destroy Jerusalem. (Josephus) (Tirinus)"],["haydock",41,null,"haydock","My father. He has no regard for this mother. (Menochius) — Her love for Jacob filled him with greater indignation; and he resolved to murder him, in order, perhaps, to revenge himself on both. Though this cruel resolution was taken in his heart, with full deliberation, he was not so careful to conceal his intentions; but his watchful mother discovered it, and by her prudence, preserved him from committing the external sin: and Jacob from falling a prey to this second Cain."],["haydock",45,null,"haydock","Both my sons. Esau would have forfeited his life for murder, chap. ix. 6. (Haydock) — Perhaps she might also fear that Jacob, in his own defence, should, in the very agony of death, give the aggressor a mortal wound; or that Esau, at least, would be forced to flee his country. Indeed, she considered him already as a lost man, on account of his marriage with the two women of Chanaan, and his savage manners. (Calmet)"],["haydock",46,null,"haydock","To live. Life will be a burden to me. (Menochius) — She does not mention the principal reason of her desiring Jacob to go to Haran, for fear of grieving the tender heart of her husband; who, it seems, knew not the temper of Esau so well as she did. (Calmet)"],["challoner",19,null,"challoner","I am Esau thy firstborn.... St. Augustine (L. Contra mendacium, c. 10), treating at large upon this place, excuseth Jacob from a lie, because this whole passage was mysterious, as relating to the preference which was afterwards to be given to the Gentiles before the carnal Jews, which Jacob by prophetic light might understand. So far is certain, that the first birthright, both by divine election and by Esau’s free cession belonged to Jacob: so that if there were any lie in the case, it could be no more than an officious and venial one."],["challoner",36,null,"challoner","Jacob.... That is, a supplanter."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"augustine":["Augustine of Hippo",1,1,430,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#augustine"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[5,10,0,"Commentary on Revelation",null,0,892,"… So Rebecca said to her son Jacob: Bring me two of the best young goats, and I will make from them dishes for your father, such as he likes to eat; and he will bless you before the Lord, before he dies (Gen. 27:9). He went and brought them, and gave them to his mother. She prepared the food, as she knew his father liked it, and she dressed him in Esau’s clothes, which she had at home with her; and she wrapped the skins of the young goats around his hands and covered his bare neck, and she gave him the stew and the bread she had baked, and handed them over. …"],[11,null,1,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 4 -- On Faith- First Discourse on Simplicity",1,523,"And that no man may imagine that his simplicity was natural foolishness, see how attentive he was to the curses of his father, and how he returned answer concerning the things which were set forth by knowledge, \"Behold, Esau my brother is a hairy man, and I am a smooth man. Peradventure my father will feel me, and I shall be in his eyes as a mocker, and curses shall come upon me and not blessings. …"],[15,17,0,"Commentary on Revelation",null,0,892,"… These are the finest garments which the Jews left at home, that is, in the Old Testament, because they were unwilling to observe them. Clothed and adorned with these, the Gentile people went to God the Father with the food of good works. And together with the foods he also brought breads. By bread the word of God is often signified, because that person fittingly goes to God the Father with the food of good works who also does not cease to admonish all others he can to perform good works. But we must also ask why the mother covered the hands of Jacob and his bare neck with goatskins. …"],[16,null,2,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part II","17. The Second Conference of Abbot Joseph",2,435,"… Towhich end the patriarch Jacob also had regard when he was not afraid to imitate the hairy appearance of his brother's body by wrapping himself up in skins, and to his credit acquiesced in his mother's instigation of a lie for this object. …"],[19,null,2,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part II","17. The Second Conference of Abbot Joseph",2,435,"… For the fruits of his labour will not be repaid to him according to the actual result, but according to what he wanted to do, and believed that he would accomplish. And again, what could there be more culpable than craft and deceit shown even to a stranger, not to mention one's brother and father? And yet the patriarch Jacob not only met with no condemnation or blame for such things but was actually dowered with the everlasting heritage of the blessing. …"],[25,27,0,"Commentary on Revelation",null,0,892,"After Isaac had taken food, Jacob also offered him wine. If the works of the active life are signified by food, we can understand by wine, which alienates the minds of men, the contemplative life, which renders the minds of certain of the elect insensitive to love of the world. Therefore, after food and drink had been taken, Isaac blessed Jacob; for those receive the blessing of the heavenly homeland from God who strive to please him with good works until the end of their life."],[25,null,1,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 11 -- On Abstinence",1,523,"Now although this meat is a burden unto the wise and prudent, yet was it given by promise unto Noah, and it was sent unto Elijah in a gift, and Abraham received God and His angels thereby, and Isaac was pleased to pour out blessings upon Jacob thereby, and Samuel offered this gift beforehand to Saul as to a king, and David and all the righteous kings made use of such meat, and it was employed by all the righteous; and they were not blamed therefor, because they were superior to lust. And they did not eat like slaves with lust, but they made use of every thing with authority like free men."],[29,null,3,"The Christian Topography","Book 5",3,550,"Let peoples serve thee, and let their princes bow down to thee, and be thou Lord over thy brethren, and let thy father's sons bow down to thee; cursed be every one that curseth thee, and blessed be every one that blesseth thee. But we see not all these things accomplished upon Jacob, but see rather that Jacob, having prostrated himself seven times upon the ground, made obeisance to Esau. And thirty kings, sprung from Esau, reigned before ever a king reigned in Israel; so that these blessings await Him who was expected to descend from them, namely, the Lord Christ, whom the whole scope of divine scripture has in view."],[36,null,1,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 11 -- On Abstinence",1,523,"Now this is the first lust which conquered the world, and because of it the first transgression of the law took place. It dismissed from Esau his birthright and his blessings. For the lust of the belly is the captain of the host of the left side, and to it are fettered all the hosts of sin, and as captains of hosts go forth to war at the head of their companies against the enemy, even so also doth it, as the captain of the host of all wickedness, go forth to war against that which is good."],[41,45,4,"First Epistle to the Corinthians","Clement's First Letter to the Corinthians, Chapters 3-4",4,99,"… And Cain said to Abel his brother, Let us go into the field. And it came to pass, while they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him. [Genesis 4:3-8] You see, brethren, how envy and jealousy led to the murder of a brother. Through envy, also, our father Jacob fled from the face of Esau his brother [Genesis 27:41-45]. Envy made Joseph be persecuted unto death, and to come into bondage. [Genesis 37:18-28] Envy compelled Moses to flee from the face of Pharaoh king of Egypt, when he heard these words from his fellow-countryman, \"Who made you a judge or a ruler over us? …"]],"fau":[["berengaudus","Berengaudus",0,"medieval",null,0],["philoxenus-of-mabbug","Philoxenus of Mabbug",0,"oriental",null,0],["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435],["cosmas-indicopleustes","Cosmas Indicopleustes",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0],["clement-of-rome","Clement of Rome",0,"father",null,0]],"ftr":[["John Litteral, released into the public domain"],["E. A. Wallis Budge, The Discourses of Philoxenus (1894)"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"],["J. W. McCrindle, Hakluyt Society (1897)"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 1 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf01"]]}